This folder themes MediaWiki core's deprecated jquery.ui module for the Notion skin. When
complete it will hold the 14 stylesheets skin.json declares, plus an images/ directory,
DEPRECATED and this file, and nothing else. Read DEPRECATED first: it records why the module is
frozen, what may and may not be changed here, and how much of the 14-file set exists so far.
Neither DEPRECATED nor this file is referenced from skin.json, and neither is ever served by
ResourceLoader.

CURRENT STATE. Two stylesheets exist -- jquery.ui.core.css and jquery.ui.theme.css -- along with
the five files in images/. The other twelve (accordion, autocomplete, button, datepicker, dialog,
menu, progressbar, resizable, selectable, slider, tabs, tooltip) are still to be authored. This
ledger is written as the specification for all 14, so entries below that name a file not yet
present describe what that file must carry when it is written, not a deviation already in place.
Each such entry is the requirement to implement; do not read the ledger as an inventory of work
finished.

The stylesheets are jQuery UI 1.9.2 -- jquery.ui.button.css is 1.11.2-alpha -- recoloured to
the Notion palette. Below is every deviation they carry or must carry: the ones inherited from
the copy in mediawiki-skins-Vector/skinStyles/jquery.ui/ that this one was adapted from, and the
ones that are new here. None of them is cosmetic. Each fixes something, and removing it
reintroduces the bug it fixed. The @noflip annotations and the RTL dialog-placement fix for
T344467 in particular must survive any future edit to these files.

jquery.ui.button.css
* Picked from jQuery UI 1.11.2-alpha instead of 1.9.2.
* Extra customizations.
* Notion: the coloured button variants those customizations add -- .ui-button-green,
  .ui-button-blue, .ui-button-red and the disabled state -- keep their selectors and their
  !important declarations, and are recoloured. Their linear-gradient() fills become flat
  background colours.
* Notion: the "white icons for colored buttons" rule keeps
  url(images/ui-icons_ffffff_256x240.png) and its @embed. It is the only url() in this file.

jquery.ui.datepicker.css
* Add @noflip to prevent CSSJanus flipping.
  Eleven rules carry it, one for each .ui-datepicker-rtl rule. Stylesheets here are authored
  LTR and flipped mechanically by CSSJanus; those eleven rules are already the RTL case, so
  flipping them would cancel them out and break the RTL calendar.

jquery.ui.dialog.css
* Extra customizations.
* Ia48990a7f3 Fix jQuery UI's dialog placement in RTL
  https://phabricator.wikimedia.org/T344467
  The fix is the @noflip annotation on "left: 0" in the .ui-dialog rule. jQuery UI positions a
  dialog at runtime by writing left into the element's own style attribute; if CSSJanus flips
  the stylesheet's "left: 0" into "right: 0" the two disagree and the dialog is placed
  off-screen in RTL. That annotation is the only @noflip in this file. Do not remove it, and do
  not reformat the .ui-dialog rule in a way that separates it from the declaration it guards.
* Notion: the titlebar customization loses its gradient tile and becomes a flat fill, so
  images/titlebar-fade.png is not carried here. See "Notion skin" below.
* Notion: the close glyph keeps url(images/close.png) and its @embed; the PNG itself is
  recoloured to Notion's secondary text colour #787774. After the titlebar change it is the
  only url() left in this file.

jquery.ui.resizable.css
* Add @noflip to prevent CSSJanus flipping.
  Six rules carry it: .ui-resizable-e, -w, -se, -sw, -nw and -ne. .ui-resizable-n and -s are
  deliberately left unannotated -- they are direction-neutral, so flipping them is harmless.

jquery.ui.theme.css
* Add @embed instructions for CSSMin.
* Change font-size from 1.0em to 0.8em.
* Join ".ui-icon", ".ui-widget-content .ui-icon" and ".ui-widget-header .ui-icon" rules
  to optimise image embedding.
* Removed ".ui-widget-content a { color: #362b36; }" and
  ".ui-widget-header a { color: #222222; }" due to bug T85857.
  They remain absent here, deliberately. Do not restore them: they recolour every link inside
  every jQuery UI widget, which is what T85857 reported.
* Notion: .ui-widget takes an Inter-first font stack in place of "sans-serif" -- Inter, then
  system UI faces, then the Codex sans stack. No webfont is bundled: MediaWiki has no
  client-side build step, and a font file would breach the skin's bundle-size budget, so on a
  machine without Inter installed these widgets render in the fallback face. That is an
  accepted fidelity compromise, not an omission. The 0.8em font-size above is kept.
* Notion: the surface and interaction-state rules are recoloured -- .ui-widget-content to the
  white canvas #ffffff, .ui-widget-header and .ui-state-default to the warm off-white
  surface #f7f6f3, .ui-state-hover and .ui-state-focus to #efefee, .ui-state-active
  to #e8e7e4, and all of their text to #37352f. Borders become 1px hairlines: #e3e2de on the
  two surfaces and on .ui-state-default, and the derived base border #d3d1cb on the
  hover/focus state, which needs to read against its own darker fill. The 3px corner radius is
  unchanged, vendor prefixes included.
* Notion: .ui-state-active takes a #696866 border rather than that #d3d1cb hairline. Selected and
  active is a PERSISTENT state -- the selected tab, the chosen date, the current menu row -- and
  its #e8e7e4 fill is only 1.12:1 against the white .ui-widget-content it sits in, so the border
  is the whole indicator. #d3d1cb reaches 1.53:1 against white and 1.23:1 against that fill, well
  under the 3:1 WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.11 asks of the visual information that identifies a component's
  state; #696866, the skin's corrected secondary text colour, measures 5.57:1 and 4.50:1, and
  cannot be mistaken for the hover state one rule above. Only the colour changed: the rule keeps
  upstream's border shorthand, its fill, its font-weight and its selector list.
* Notion: the .ui-state-default / -hover / -active anchor rules take #37352f, the text
  colour -- NOT a link blue. Those selectors style control labels (button, tab and menu-item
  text), which Notion renders in dark text and lets the fill change carry, reserving blue for
  genuine links. It is also the only contrast-safe reading: measured against the #f7f6f3
  .ui-state-default fill, the link blue #487ca5 reaches 4.13:1 and the icon blue #337ea9
  4.14:1, both short of the 4.5:1 WCAG AA asks of normal-size text, and .ui-widget text is
  0.8em. #37352f reaches 11.3:1 on the same fill. There is consequently no #487ca5 and no
  #2eaadc anywhere in this file. Upstream's "text-decoration: none" is kept.
* Notion, ADDITIVE RULE: a second
  ".ui-state-focus, .ui-widget-content .ui-state-focus, .ui-widget-header .ui-state-focus"
  rule is appended immediately after the shared hover/focus rule, giving focus a #337ea9
  border-color plus a matching 1px outline at outline-offset: 1px. Upstream shares one rule
  between hover and focus, which works while both are a tinted blue but not once hover is a
  quiet neutral fill -- a keyboard user would be left with no indicator at all, and
  accessibility is not negotiable here. It is appended rather than merged into the shared
  rule's selector list, so that rule stays directly comparable with upstream. #337ea9
  measures 4.47:1 on #ffffff, 4.14:1 on #f7f6f3 and 3.89:1 on the #efefee hover fill, clearing
  the 3:1 WCAG AA asks of a non-text indicator wherever it can land, while the hover border
  #d3d1cb sits at 1.33:1 against its own fill -- the two states cannot be confused. The
  outline is load-bearing, not decoration: .ui-state-active follows with a border shorthand
  that would otherwise reset the focus border on a control that is both selected and focused,
  which is exactly the case -- a focused active tab -- where the indicator matters most.
  Known and accepted: jQuery UI also applies ui-state-focus to the active row of a menu or an
  autocomplete on mouseenter, so that row draws the ring too.
* Notion: .ui-state-highlight stays a yellow cue rather than being folded into the neutral
  palette, because the state is semantic. Background is Notion's published yellow tint
  #fbf3db with #37352f text (11.1:1). The tint alone is 1.11:1 against the white
  .ui-widget-content it sits in, so the cue has to be carried by its border, and that border is
  #a58112: derived from the tint by holding the tint's hue (45deg) and saturation (80%) and
  dropping lightness from 92% to 36%, which keeps the cue yellow where a plain channel-scaled
  darkening turns it beige. It measures 3.65:1 against white and 3.30:1 against the tint it
  encloses, clear of the 3:1 WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.11 minimum on both sides. An earlier revision of
  this file used #f1d47e there, at 1.45:1 and 1.34:1 -- the same order of presence as the
  neutral #d3d1cb hairline, and not reliably perceivable; it is recorded here because the
  correction is the deviation from upstream, not the colour it replaced. Like #d3d1cb this is a
  derived value, not a published Notion colour, and the derivation is stated in the file.
* Notion: .ui-state-error keeps a solid, saturated red with white text rather than being
  softened to a light tint. Its icons come from ui-icons_ffffff_256x240.png, so a pale
  background would leave both the text and the icons illegible. The red is #b3352f: Notion's
  published #d44c47 carries white text at only 4.26:1, so it is darkened until the pair
  measures 6.06:1, clear of WCAG AA for normal-size text. Border and background take the same
  value, as upstream had them. .ui-state-error-text keeps white text, which is upstream's own
  ThemeRoller output for this theme (fcError=ffffff): that class is meant for text on an error
  surface. No fifth sprite is introduced for a dark error-text variant.
* Notion: .ui-widget-overlay is a light veil, recoloured to Notion's warm off-white --
  "background: rgba(247, 246, 243, 0.6); opacity: 1; filter: none". Stock jQuery UI darkens
  the page behind a modal; Vector's copy had already replaced that with
  "rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5); opacity: 1; filter: none" without recording it, which is why
  ui-bg_flat_100_000000_40x100.png sits in Vector's images/ referenced from nowhere at all.
  The light-veil semantic is deliberately preserved here, since Notion separates a modal from
  the page with the dialog's own hairline and elevation rather than by darkening; the deviation
  and its recolour are recorded, and the tile is not carried. "opacity: 1; filter: none" must
  stay: the alpha lives in the colour, so the element must not be faded a second time.
* Notion: .ui-widget-shadow loses its background image and becomes a flat low-alpha shadow
  colour, #0f0f0f, Notion's warm shadow base. Two changes are folded together here and they must
  be judged separately.
  Dropping the tile is safe: upstream painted the rule with ui-bg_flat_70_000000_40x100.png, a
  40x100 PNG of one uniform value, so a flat background-color of the same colour would render
  identically. The tile is not carried, so the url() and its @embed had to go with it or the
  reference would dangle.
  Changing the colour is a VISUAL change, not an equivalence. Measured rather than inferred from
  the filename: that tile is 1-bit greyscale, colour type 0, with no alpha channel and no tRNS
  chunk, and all 4000 of its pixels are black -- fully opaque #000000. The "70" in the
  ThemeRoller filename is the opacity setting used to generate it, not the pixel alpha; the fade
  comes from the CSS opacity: .2. So the veil moves from neutral black to warm near-black, which
  is the intended retheme and not a like-for-like substitution.
  The geometry (margin: -7px 0 0 -7px; padding: 7px), the opacity: .2, the legacy
  filter:Alpha(Opacity=20) declaration and the vendor-prefixed border-radius properties all stay;
  the 8px radius is additionally an exact match for Notion's panel radius, so it needs no change.
* Notion: the ".ui-dialog { border: ...; box-shadow: ...; }" rule appended at the end of this
  file is recoloured to a #e3e2de hairline and Notion's subtle elevation,
  "0 1px 4px rgba(15, 15, 15, 0.1), 0 4px 12px rgba(15, 15, 15, 0.08)", in place of Vector's
  single hard "0 2px 2px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25)" over a #a2a9b1 border. Vector appended that
  rule without recording it either; jQuery UI's own ThemeRoller output contains no .ui-dialog
  rule in this file at all, so it is a local addition in both skins. The two-stop shadow is
  written as a literal because Codex exposes only single-stop drop shadows and this folder sits
  outside the token regime; that licenses nothing outside this folder.

jquery.ui.progressbar.css
* Notion: .ui-progressbar .ui-progressbar-value is given an explicit accent fill, #196f92 -- the
  skin's corrected accent, NOT Notion's published #2eaadc. Upstream gives the value bar no
  background of its own -- jQuery UI paints it by putting the ui-widget-header class on it -- and
  once ui-widget-header becomes the warm off-white #f7f6f3 the fill would be invisible against the
  white ui-widget-content track. The fill is the only thing that conveys how far the bar has
  filled, so it is a graphical object and needs 3:1 against that track: #196f92 measures 5.63:1
  where #2eaadc reaches only 2.66:1. It is exactly the single-declaration fill DEPRECATED permits
  adding for a control a recolour would otherwise render invisible, and not a licence to restyle
  the widget.

jquery.ui.slider.css
* Notion: .ui-slider .ui-slider-range is given the same explicit #196f92 fill, for the same two
  reasons -- it too is painted by ui-widget-header upstream and would otherwise disappear against
  the track, and it too carries state that has to be perceivable.

jquery.ui.tooltip.css
* Notion: .ui-tooltip trades the grey blur "box-shadow: 0 0 5px #aaa" for Notion's subtle
  elevation, and the "body .ui-tooltip" border-width drops from 2px to the 1px hairline used
  throughout this skin. padding, position, z-index and max-width are untouched.

jquery.ui.selectable.css
* Notion: the .ui-selectable-helper marquee border changes from "1px dotted black" to a
  dotted #787774. Pure black is not in the Notion palette.

jquery.ui.core.css, jquery.ui.accordion.css, jquery.ui.autocomplete.css, jquery.ui.menu.css,
jquery.ui.tabs.css
* Carried across structurally unchanged. They are layout and behaviour only; the colour they
  display comes from jquery.ui.theme.css, so recolouring that file restyles them. Listed here
  so the absence of an entry is understood as "checked, nothing to patch" rather than
  "overlooked".

Notion skin:
* Colour values in this folder are written as literals, not as design tokens. These are plain
  .css files: they cannot @import 'mediawiki.skin.variables.less', and the folder is excluded
  from stylelint by .stylelintignore. This is the single documented exception to the skin's
  zero-hardcoded-values rule. The twelve .less overrides in the parent skinStyles/ directory do
  use tokens, and nothing here licenses a literal anywhere else in the skin. The literals used
  are the Notion palette exactly as resources/mediawiki.less/notion/mediawiki.skin.variables.less
  holds it, which for four of them is a value corrected for contrast rather than the one Notion
  publishes -- that file records each published origin, derivation and measured ratio, and this
  folder must not drift from it: primary text #37352f, secondary text #696866 (published #787774,
  darkened along its own hue to clear WCAG AA), tertiary #9b9a97 (kept as published; it styles
  only inactive controls, which WCAG exempts), canvas #ffffff, secondary surface #f7f6f3,
  hover #efefee, active #e8e7e4, divider #e3e2de, hairline #e9e9e7, base border #d3d1cb,
  accent #196f92 (published #2eaadc, darkened the same way), link #3f6c90 (published #487ca5,
  likewise). Two published blues still appear in this file, both deliberately: #337ea9 as the
  focus ring, where 4.47:1 is comfortably above the 3:1 a non-text indicator needs, and #787774 as
  the .ui-selectable-helper marquee and the close-glyph sprite, neither of which carries text.
* Gradient chrome removed. jQuery UI's gradient and inset background-image tiles are replaced
  by flat background-color declarations, because Notion's visual language is flat surfaces,
  1px hairlines and no heavy chrome. Ten of the images/ files Vector ships are consequently not
  carried here, and every rule that referenced one lost its url() and its @embed along with it:
  ui-bg_highlight-hard_100_f2f5f7_1x100.png -- was .ui-widget-content
  ui-bg_highlight-soft_100_ffffff_1x100.png -- was .ui-widget-header
  ui-bg_highlight-hard_80_d7ebf9_1x100.png -- was .ui-state-default
  ui-bg_highlight-soft_100_e4f1fb_1x100.png -- was .ui-state-hover / .ui-state-focus
  ui-bg_inset-hard_100_f0f0f0_1x100.png -- was .ui-state-active
  ui-bg_highlight-soft_25_ffef8f_1x100.png -- was .ui-state-highlight
  ui-bg_flat_15_cd0a0a_40x100.png -- was .ui-state-error
  ui-bg_flat_70_000000_40x100.png -- was .ui-widget-shadow
  ui-bg_flat_100_000000_40x100.png -- referenced from nowhere in Vector's copy either
  titlebar-fade.png -- was the .ui-dialog titlebar customization
  Two things are true of the three ui-bg_flat_* tiles and they must not be conflated. Each is
  40x100 pixels of ONE uniform, fully opaque colour -- measured: the two 000000 tiles are 1-bit
  greyscale with no alpha channel and every pixel black, and the cd0a0a tile is a single-entry
  palette of rgb(205, 10, 10), also with no alpha. Because they carry no gradient and no
  transparency, replacing the image with a background-color renders the same thing PROVIDED the
  colour is the same, which is what makes dropping the url() safe.
  It is not the same colour in two of the three cases, and that is deliberate. .ui-widget-shadow
  moves from opaque black to #0f0f0f and .ui-state-error from rgb(205, 10, 10) to #b3352f: both
  are intended retheming, visible on screen, and neither should be described as changing nothing.
  Only ui-bg_flat_100_000000_40x100.png truly changes nothing, because no rule referenced it.
  Note also that the numeric segment of a ThemeRoller filename is the opacity setting used to
  generate the file, not the pixel alpha -- ui-bg_flat_70_000000 is fully opaque, not 70% black.
  The six 1x100 strips -- the five highlight-* tiles and the inset-hard tile -- and the 1x36
  titlebar fade each encoded a vertical gradient, and losing it is the intended flattening rather
  than an oversight.
* Icon sprite set reduced from six variants to four. Only the url() values changed: no selector
  was joined or split beyond the join Vector already made, all six icon rules remain, and each
  keeps its @embed. The complete mapping is:
  images/ui-icons_787774_256x240.png (Notion secondary text) -- the joined
    ".ui-icon, .ui-widget-content .ui-icon, .ui-widget-header .ui-icon" rule, and
    ".ui-state-default .ui-icon"
  images/ui-icons_337ea9_256x240.png (Notion icon blue) -- ".ui-state-hover .ui-icon,
    .ui-state-focus .ui-icon"
  images/ui-icons_37352f_256x240.png (Notion primary text) -- ".ui-state-active .ui-icon" and
    ".ui-state-highlight .ui-icon"
  images/ui-icons_ffffff_256x240.png -- ".ui-state-error .ui-icon, .ui-state-error-text
    .ui-icon" in jquery.ui.theme.css, and the coloured-button rule in jquery.ui.button.css
  Not carried, being the discarded variants of the folder this was adapted from:
  ui-icons_72a7cf_256x240.png, ui-icons_3d80b3_256x240.png, ui-icons_2694e8_256x240.png,
  ui-icons_666666_256x240.png and ui-icons_2e83ff_256x240.png. ui-icons_ffffff_256x240.png is the
  one sprite whose filename is unchanged. All four kept sprites are 256x240, so no
  background-position in the sprite grid moved.
  Worth knowing before assuming a dark glyph needs a new asset: ui-icons_37352f_256x240.png IS
  one of the four already carried, so any rule that wants a dark icon can point at it without an
  addition to images/.
* Annotation TARGETS for the completed folder, and the count today. When all 14 stylesheets exist
  the folder must carry 18 @noflip -- 11 in jquery.ui.datepicker.css, 6 in
  jquery.ui.resizable.css, 1 in jquery.ui.dialog.css -- and 8 @embed, one on each surviving image
  rule: 6 in jquery.ui.theme.css, 1 in jquery.ui.dialog.css, 1 in jquery.ui.button.css. Of those,
  the 6 @embed in jquery.ui.theme.css are in place now; the remaining 2 @embed and all 18 @noflip
  live in files not yet written, since jquery.ui.core.css legitimately carries neither. Verify
  against the target only once the file in question exists.
* Approved structural deviations from upstream ThemeRoller output, so that "colour, not structure"
  is not read as "no structure at all". These are sanctioned and each is described in full in its
  own entry above:
  - jquery.ui.theme.css appends a .ui-dialog rule (border plus box-shadow) that ThemeRoller does
    not emit at all. It is a local addition, inherited from the copy this folder was adapted from.
  - The two ".ui-widget-content a" / ".ui-widget-header a" colour rules are REMOVED rather than
    recoloured (T85857).
  - images/ carries a deliberately smaller and differently named sprite set than the folder it
    was adapted from: five files, not sixteen, and only ui-icons_ffffff_256x240.png keeps its
    upstream name. Every dropped background tile took its url() and @embed with it.
  Beyond those, nothing is restructured: selectors, rule order, the vendor-prefixed border-radius
  properties, the filter:Alpha(Opacity=...) and zoom: 1 declarations, the ::-moz-focus-inner
  resets and the IE6 .ui-datepicker-cover iframe fix are all upstream's and are kept deliberately.
* To re-check this ledger against the stylesheets, run from this directory:
    ls *.css
    grep -c @noflip jquery.ui.datepicker.css jquery.ui.resizable.css jquery.ui.dialog.css
    grep -n "url(" *.css
    grep -c @embed jquery.ui.theme.css jquery.ui.dialog.css jquery.ui.button.css
    grep -n "ui-widget-content a\|ui-widget-header a" jquery.ui.theme.css
    ls images/
  The first shows which of the 14 exist, and the grep commands can only be run against those.
  Once a file exists its counts must match the targets above: datepicker 11, resizable 6, dialog
  1; theme 6, dialog 1, button 1. Every filename the url() command prints must appear both in the
  sprite mapping above and in the output of the last command. The ui-widget-content/header grep
  must print nothing, because those two colour rules stay removed (T85857). If the stylesheets
  and this file ever disagree, the stylesheets are what ship: correct whichever of the two is
  wrong, but do not leave them disagreeing.

